---
// What the site does with personal data. Every line describes something the code actually does; change it in the
// same commit as the behaviour. Google needs this page's URL to let the website send email (README.md#gmail).
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHeader from "../components/PageHeader.astro";
import { getSettings } from "../lib/sanity/content";

const s = await getSettings();
const email = s.contactEmail;
---

<Base title="Privacy" description="What the Battersea Cougars website and team app do with your details.">
  <PageHeader eyebrow="Small print" title="Privacy" lede="Short, because the site collects very little." />
  <div class="wrap page-body">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Who we are</h2>
      <p>
        Battersea Cougars Inline Hockey Club, a roller hockey club in South West London. Questions about your details go
        to <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>What we collect</h2>
      <p>
        Only what you send us through the <a href="/join/">Try a session</a> form: your name, email address, and, if you
        give them, your phone number, your hockey experience and your message. We use them to reply to you and help you
        get started at the club, and for nothing else. We don't sell or share them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your details are emailed to the club's inbox, and the club replies to you from it. That email goes through
        Google's Gmail.
      </p>

      <h2>Players on this website</h2>
      <p>
        The <a href="/team/">team page</a> and the <a href="/results/">tournament results</a> show the players' names,
        and who played, scored and won. Each player goes by the name they chose in the club's team app: their first name
        and initial unless they picked otherwise. Nobody's full name is shown unless they choose it, and a tournament
        the club keeps off the website isn't shown.
      </p>

      <h2>Members: the team app</h2>
      <p>
        Members use the club's team app to sign up for training and tournaments, and the club uses it to run them and to
        keep track of fees. For that it holds your name, the name you go by, your email address (to sign in), your phone
        number if you give it, your position and the bio on your player card; which sessions, tournaments and events you
        signed up for and played; what you owe and have paid, and the reference your bank transfers use; and a skill
        rating the club's organisers set to balance teams, which other members don't see.
      </p>
      <p>
        Other members see the name you go by, your position, your bio and what you've signed up for. Your email and
        phone are seen only by you and the club's admins. Your sign-in emails and the club's notices go through Google's
        Gmail.
      </p>
      <p>
        To sign you in and keep you signed in, the app sets two cookies, which it needs to work, and remembers which
        browser each sign-in is on so you can tell your devices apart. Sign-in codes are kept 30 days, and a sign-in 30
        days after you sign out or it runs out. A record of who changed what in the app (who was made an admin, say) is
        kept for 2 years.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can download everything the app holds about you, or delete your account, in the app: Profile, then Your
        data. Deleting removes your name, email, phone and everything else that says who you are, straight away. What
        the club must keep for its accounts (payments) and its numbers (that someone played a session) stays, about a
        "Former member" nobody can name.
      </p>

      <h2>Where it's kept, and for how long</h2>
      <p>
        On Cloudflare, which runs this website and the team app, in Western Europe, and in the club's Gmail inbox. To
        stop spam, the site briefly remembers the internet address a form came from (for 15 minutes). We delete your
        enquiry 12 months after you send it, unless you've joined the club. A member's details are kept while they're in
        the club, until they delete their account or ask us to. The club's database is backed up every night, encrypted,
        on GitHub, and each backup is kept for 90 days, so a deleted account is gone from the backups 90 days later.
      </p>

      <h2>Cookies</h2>
      <p>
        The website itself doesn't set cookies or track you, and stores nothing in your browser (the team app's sign-in
        cookies are above). Videos come from YouTube's privacy-enhanced player, and only load when you press play.
      </p>
      <p>
        To keep spam bots out, the <a href="/join/">Try a session</a> form uses Cloudflare Turnstile, which checks your
        browser looks like a person's (
        <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/turnstile-privacy-policy/" rel="noopener">
          Cloudflare's Turnstile privacy notice
        </a>
        ). If it says you're a person, we email you once to confirm we've got your details.
      </p>

      <h2>Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        You can ask to see, correct or delete the details we hold about you: email{" "}
        <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a>. If you're unhappy with how we've handled them, the
        <a href="https://ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint/" rel="noopener">
          Information Commissioner's Office
        </a>{" "}
        takes complaints from anyone in the UK.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
